From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 10:31:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] fix(tiler): scale the tile limits in 64 bits where usize
 has 32

tippecanoe scales the byte and feature limits, and the sizes it checks
against them, by the counts of kept and skipped features in its 64-bit
size_t. In a 32-bit usize, as on WebAssembly, the products wrap at a few
thousand features and the 500 kilobyte limit drops to a few bytes, so
every tile drops almost all of its features. The products are now made in
u64, which changes nothing where usize has 64 bits.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018JFwoob6fHaD4i2xGpSuk7
---
 src/tiler/make.rs | 43 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------------
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/tiler/make.rs b/src/tiler/make.rs
index 688d942..ac2f9b0 100644
--- a/src/tiler/make.rs
+++ b/src/tiler/make.rs
@@ -35,6 +35,14 @@ use super::util::*;
 use crate::store::Attr;
 use tippecanoe::plugin::filter_layers;
 
+/// `a * b / c` as tippecanoe computes it in its 64-bit `size_t`, wrapping as
+/// it does there. Where `usize` has 32 bits (WebAssembly), the product would
+/// wrap far sooner and shrink the size and feature limits to almost nothing.
+fn mul_div(a: usize, b: usize, c: usize) -> usize {
+    let r = (a as u64).wrapping_mul(b as u64) / c as u64;
+    usize::try_from(r).unwrap_or(usize::MAX)
+}
+
